Fog of war for the map window (Phase 37 of the Copilot / Shattered-Maps plan; docs/shattered-maps/surfaces.md "Fog of war"). A PRODUCTION feature, off by default (setting `fog` in ../settings.ts).

visitRecorder.ts is bound once in pages/MainContainer.tsx and records ALWAYS (the owner's answer: so turning fog on later shows past exploration), whether or not the window is open: it runs the shared map session on each game:room-data and writes a visit ONLY when the answer is exactly one room with the same exits (never a guess, lookalikes or an unmapped room). visits.ts is the store (keys `visit|world|account|character|room` in the page's mine store, ids only, read once per world+account, a copy handed out so callers cannot change it). identity.ts says whose it is: the account id the token carries when it carries one, else "local" (the site's device tokens are opaque, so today the user level is everything this browser has seen; a real account id is a prerequisite of Phase 44), and the character the game last named (a visit before that counts for the user level only). useVisited.ts feeds the window; the picture itself is drawn from core's applyFog (services/shattered-maps-core/src/fog.ts), which leaves out unseen rooms and turns exits to them into `?` stubs.

Worlds never mix (every call names its world). Tests that render the window pass their own VisitStore; the recorder test mocks features/auth/siteApi (import.meta.env is not supported by the jest transform).

Tests of this folder sit beside each file (*.test.ts); the window-level behaviour (the Add this room button, fog drawing, the eye) is in components/WorldMapWindow.test.tsx and components/AddRoomPanel.test.tsx, which pass their own stores instead of the shared IndexedDB ones.

visits.ts byCharacter/addMany feed the cloud backup (../sync/backup.ts): addMany adds under the CURRENT browser's account key, whatever characters the site returns, and writes in groups of 500 because one IndexedDB transaction per room made a 24,400-room restore take about 11 s (measured). The browser account key stays "local" for opaque tokens; the backup does not change that.
